{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfgM8objKrRaGS9WUnH4W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote a new model for this repair slot, `person_pattern_sensitivity_rule_built`, and it passes the self-check. It hasn't yet been through the novelty check against the other models, which is the gate the last attempt failed.\n\n**Why the last attempt was rejected:** it added a per-person lapse rate to the current best model, `rule_built_tally_switch_triplet`. The live model `personal_engagement_lapse_tally_switch` already does that, so the two made almost identical predictions (difference 0.00015, against a required 0.002). Two other live models, `person_decisiveness_rule_built_tally` and `shared_impression_personal_decisiveness`, already explain the critique's finding as people differing in decision noise. So I picked a different source of differences between people.\n\n**New hypothesis:** I kept everything in the best model. The one change is that each person now has their own sensitivity to pattern structure. This single per-person factor scales the two structural cues together: the reward for varied three-flip chunks and the penalty for sequences built by a copying or mirror rule. Some people then reliably reject sequences that repeat the same chunks or follow a rule, while others hardly notice and judge only by the heads-minus-tails tally and how often the coin switches. That explains the critique's finding (it survives the false-discovery correction) that people differ more than the best model allows in how often they agree with the majority. It places those differences in what people perceive rather than in noise or lapses. In the code, the factor is `exp(sigma_pat * z_pat)`, defined for 400 participant slots with a median of 1, so the shared weights still describe the typical person.\n\n**Self-check result:** `OK`. A short 100-draw, single-chain test fit gave a finite ELPD-LOO of -3279.9. With on